-- Q4484: Terms of Engagement [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The auditor provides an absolute guarantee that all fraud will be detected","The auditor investigates fraud only when specifically requested by the client","Fraud detection is solely a management responsibility, not the auditor''s concern","The audit provides reasonable assurance of detecting material misstatements from fraud or error"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under AU-C 210 and AU-C 240, the engagement letter should clarify that the audit is designed to provide reasonable assurance about whether the financial statements are free from material misstatement, whether caused by fraud or error. This sets appropriate expectations without guaranteeing detection of all fraud.
Wrong (A): Auditing standards do not provide a guarantee of fraud detection; reasonable assurance acknowledges inherent audit limitations.
Wrong (B): The auditor has a responsibility to consider fraud risk on every engagement, not only when the client requests it.
Wrong (C): While management has primary responsibility for fraud prevention and detection, the auditor also has responsibilities under AU-C 240.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4484;

-- Q4488: Understanding the Entity and Environment [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The most efficient staffing plan for the current audit engagement","Business risks that may result in material misstatement of the financial statements","The overall effectiveness of the entity''s marketing and sales strategy","The approach that the predecessor auditor used for the prior engagement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 315, understanding the entity''s objectives and strategies helps the auditor identify business risks — risks arising from significant conditions, events, or circumstances that could adversely affect the entity''s ability to achieve its objectives. A strategic shift like moving to a subscription model may create new risks of material misstatement if financial statements do not reflect the changed business reality.
Wrong (A): Staffing decisions are an administrative matter, not the primary purpose of understanding entity strategy.
Wrong (C): Marketing effectiveness is an operational concern that is not directly relevant to identifying financial statement misstatement risks.
Wrong (D): The predecessor''s approach may inform the audit but is not identified through understanding the entity''s strategies.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4488;

-- Q4490: Understanding the Entity and Environment [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The entity''s marketing strategies and competitive positioning plans","The entity''s employee vacation and leave policy administration","Significant decisions, commitments, and events affecting the financial statements","The entity''s IT infrastructure architecture and system configurations"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under AU-C 315, reviewing board minutes helps the auditor understand significant decisions, commitments, and events that may affect the financial statements. These include authorization of major transactions, litigation developments, dividend declarations, related party transactions, and changes in accounting policies or management.
Wrong (A): Marketing strategies are operational matters not typically documented in board minutes for financial reporting purposes.
Wrong (B): Vacation and leave policies are routine HR matters, not the type of significant decisions that board minutes reveal to auditors.
Wrong (D): IT infrastructure details are typically obtained through other risk assessment procedures, not through board minute review.$EXPL$
